#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Plugin SessionStart bootstrap — injects the getff entry-point context so the
# using-getff skill auto-triggers without a manual Skill invocation.
# Invoked via run-hook.cmd from plugin/hooks/hooks.json (matcher: startup|clear|compact).
# @cc-only-rationale: SessionStart is a CC-native fire-point; this plugin hook is CC+ZCode
#   dual-harness (inline _emit_bootstrap branches on ZCODE_PROJECT_DIR), but has no SEPARATE
#   portable counterpart artifact — one file serves both harnesses via inline branching, so
#   @dual-pair (which requires a distinct counterpart) does not apply. The dual-harness logic
#   is the portability; the CC-first rationale records why no pair artifact exists.
# Under CC, plain SessionStart stdout is auto-injected into session context. Under ZCode,
# stdout must be strict-JSON {additionalContext} (plain is discarded + the run is marked
# failed). _emit_bootstrap branches on ZCODE_PROJECT_DIR (ZCode injects it; CC does not) so
# one file serves both harnesses. Off-CC/off-ZCode (OpenCode et al.) the using-getff skill is
# read on demand — the accepted degradation documented in S7 / dual-implementation-discipline §3.
# spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-22-cc-plugin-packaging-design.md §3 (#4, ADAPT of using-superpowers)
#
# Keep it short — progressive disclosure: the directive lives here, the full protocol in the skill.
set -uo pipefail

# Self-resolve our own dir (plugin-data); prefer the plugin root CC provides.
SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
PLUGIN_ROOT="${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-$(cd "$SCRIPT_DIR/.." && pwd)}"

# Harness-portable output: CC auto-injects plain stdout; ZCode needs JSON. Inlined (not sourced
# from lib/) because plugin hooks ship standalone inside the plugin payload (no lib/ sibling).
_emit_bootstrap() {
  local text="$1"
  if [ -n "${ZCODE_PROJECT_DIR:-}" ] && command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    jq -n --arg c "$text" '{additionalContext:$c}'
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$text"
  fi
}

read -r -d '' BOOTSTRAP <<BOOTSTRAP
[getff plugin — session bootstrap]
This repo has the getff plugin: turn every codebase convention into an executable
artifact that fails at the earliest reachable channel (edit-time → pre-commit → pre-push → CI
→ production). Thesis: documents lie; tests don't.

Before responding or acting — even before clarifying questions — invoke the relevant skill if
there is even a ~1% chance it applies:
  • using-getff — the entry point: how to find/use these skills + the instruction ladder.
  • getff — the 5-layer enforcement methodology (lint/CI/hooks, architecture, meta-tests,
    spec-by-example, mutation, living docs); trigger on any "make my code resist AI drift" ask.
Invoke via the Skill tool (do not Read the skill file to "use" it).

Instruction priority: this project's own CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md / direct user requests WIN over
the plugin's skills, which win over default behaviour. The plugin supplies discipline; the repo
stays in control.

Honest boundary: this plugin delivers the SOFT layer (skills/agents/session hooks). The HARD
layer (git hooks + CI that fail the build) is opt-in via /getff:install-enforcement —
a plugin never silently mutates your git/CI.

Skills live under: ${PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/
[/getff plugin]
BOOTSTRAP

_emit_bootstrap "$BOOTSTRAP"
